  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 13, $j$4.1 Graphing Linear Inequalities$j$, $j${"body": "A linear inequality in two variables, such as y > 2x − 1, has a whole region of solutions.\n\nSteps to graph: (1) Graph the boundary line. Use a solid line for ≤ or ≥ and a dashed line for < or >. (2) Pick a test point not on the line, such as (0, 0). (3) If the test point makes the inequality true, shade its side. If false, shade the other side.\n\nWorked example: y > 2x − 1. Test (0, 0): 0 > −1 is true, so shade the side containing the origin (above the line). The line is dashed.\n\nWorked example: y < 2x − 4. Test (0, 0): 0 < −4 is false, so shade the side that does not contain the origin.\n\nWriting from words: \"the sum of x and y is at most 10\" is x + y ≤ 10.\n\nThink it through: the boundary line splits the plane into two sides, and the test point tells you which side to shade. Use (0, 0) when the line does not pass through it, because the arithmetic is easy. If the line does pass through the origin, test another point such as (1, 0). A solid line means points on the line count, so it is used with ≤ and ≥. A dashed line means they do not count, so it is used with < and >. For y ≥ 3x + 2, draw a solid line through (0, 2) with slope 3, and test (0, 0): 0 ≥ 2 is false, so shade the side away from the origin.\n\nYour task: (a) Is (0, 3) a solution of y > x + 1? (b) Describe the graph of y ≥ 3x + 2. (c) Write an inequality for \"the sum of x and y is at most 10\".\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.2.7, MA.912.AR.2.8.", "video": "", "script": "In the last unit we solved inequalities with one variable and graphed them on a number line. Today we graph inequalities with two variables, like y is greater than two x minus one. Their solutions are not a single point or a line. They are an entire region of the plane.\n\nHere is the plan. Step one, graph the boundary line. Pretend the inequality sign is an equal sign and graph the line. Step two, decide whether the line is solid or dashed. If the inequality is less than or equal to, or greater than or equal to, the points on the line are solutions, so the line is solid. If the inequality is strictly less than or greater than, the points on the line are not solutions, so the line is dashed. Step three, choose a test point that is not on the line. The origin, zero comma zero, is usually easiest. Step four, substitute the test point. If the statement is true, shade the side containing that point. If it is false, shade the other side.\n\nLet us practice. Graph y is greater than two x minus one. The boundary is the line y equals two x minus one, with y intercept negative one and slope two. The sign is strictly greater than, so the line is dashed. Test the origin: is zero greater than negative one? Yes, that is true. So we shade the side of the line that contains the origin, which is above the line.\n\nAnother one. Graph y is less than two x minus four. The line has y intercept negative four and slope two. Dashed. Test the origin: is zero less than negative four? No, that is false. So we shade the side that does not contain the origin, which is below and to the right of the line.\n\nA shortcut: when the inequality is solved for y, greater than means shade above and less than means shade below. But the test point method always works, even for vertical lines or inequalities in standard form.\n\nWe can also check whether a specific point is a solution. Is the point zero comma three a solution of y greater than x plus one? Substitute: three is greater than zero plus one, so three is greater than one. True. It is a solution.\n\nFinally, writing inequalities from words. The sum of x and y is at most ten becomes x plus y is less than or equal to ten. Phrases like at most and no more than mean less than or equal to. Phrases like at least and no fewer than mean greater than or equal to.\n\nYour task today is to test a point, describe a graph, and write an inequality from words. Then take the four question check.\n\nSolutions come in many shapes, and inequalities remind us that there is often more than one right answer. Wisdom is knowing the boundaries. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.2.7, MA.912.AR.2.8"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$4.1 Graphing Linear Inequalities$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 14, $j$4.2 Solving Systems by Graphing$j$, $j${"body": "A system of equations is two or more equations with the same variables. A solution is an ordered pair that makes every equation true. On a graph, it is where the lines intersect.\n\nWorked example: y = x + 1 and y = −x + 5. Graph both. They meet at (2, 3). Check: 3 = 2 + 1 and 3 = −2 + 5. Both true.\n\nThree cases: one solution (lines cross), no solution (parallel lines, same slope, different intercepts), infinitely many solutions (the same line).\n\nChecking: substitute the point into both equations.\n\nThink it through: graphing gives a picture of the solution, but pictures can be a little off. Always confirm the intersection by substituting into both equations. For (4, 3) in x + y = 7 and x − y = 1, we get 4 + 3 = 7 and 4 − 3 = 1, so both are true. Lines with the same slope and different intercepts never meet, so the system has no solution. Two equations that describe the same line, like y = 3x + 1 and 2y = 6x + 2, share every point, so there are infinitely many solutions. Write each equation in slope intercept form to compare them quickly.\n\nYour task: (a) Solve by graphing y = x + 1 and y = −x + 5. (b) Does (4, 3) satisfy both x + y = 7 and x − y = 1? (c) What happens with y = 2x + 3 and y = 2x − 1? (d) What happens with y = 3x + 1 and 2y = 6x + 2?\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.9.1.", "video": "", "script": "Sometimes two different conditions must be true at the same time. That is a system of equations. A system of equations is two or more equations that use the same variables. The solution of the system is an ordered pair that makes all the equations true at once.\n\nThe first method for solving a system is graphing. We graph both lines on the same coordinate plane. Any point where the two lines cross is on both lines, so it satisfies both equations. That crossing point is the solution.\n\nLet us try an example. Take y equals x plus one, and y equals negative x plus five. The first line has y intercept one and slope one. The second line has y intercept five and slope negative one. If we draw both carefully, they cross at the point two comma three.\n\nHow do we check? Substitute two for x and three for y in both equations. In the first, three equals two plus one, which is true. In the second, three equals negative two plus five, which is true. The point two comma three works in both, so it is the solution.\n\nA system can have one solution, no solution, or infinitely many solutions. If the lines cross once, there is exactly one solution. If the lines are parallel, they never meet, so there is no solution. Parallel lines have the same slope but different y intercepts. For example, y equals two x plus three and y equals two x minus one. If both equations describe the same line, then every point on the line is a solution, so there are infinitely many. For example, y equals three x plus one and two y equals six x plus two are the same line, because the second is the first multiplied by two.\n\nGraphing is a great way to see what is going on, but it has limits. If the intersection has fractions or decimals, it is hard to read from a graph. That is why we will learn algebraic methods in the next lesson.\n\nWhen you graph, use a ruler, label your axes, and always check your answer by substituting. A careful graph is a clear picture.\n\nYour task today is to solve one system by graphing, check an ordered pair, and classify two special systems. Then take the four question check.\n\nWhere two paths meet, a decision is made. In life, as in a graph, the meeting point is where things become clear. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.9.1"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$4.2 Solving Systems by Graphing$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 15, $j$4.3 Solving Systems Algebraically$j$, $j${"body": "Substitution: solve one equation for a variable, then substitute into the other.\nElimination: add or subtract equations to cancel a variable. Multiply first if needed.\n\nWorked example (substitution): y = 2x − 1 and 3x + y = 9. Then 3x + (2x − 1) = 9, so 5x = 10 and x = 2. Then y = 3. Solution (2, 3).\n\nWorked example (elimination): x + y = 10 and x − y = 4. Add: 2x = 14, so x = 7. Then y = 3. Solution (7, 3).\n\nWord problem: Adult tickets cost $8 and child tickets cost $5. 100 tickets are sold for $620. a + c = 100 and 8a + 5c = 620. Then c = 100 − a, so 8a + 500 − 5a = 620, so 3a = 120, a = 40, c = 60.\n\nThink it through: choose the method that fits. When one equation already has a variable alone, as in y = 2x − 1, use substitution. When the equations line up with matching or opposite coefficients, use elimination. Put the whole expression in parentheses when you substitute, so signs do not get lost. Example of elimination: x + y = 9 and x − y = 1. Add the equations to get 2x = 10, so x = 5, and then y = 4. Always check the pair in both original equations. In a word problem, name your two unknowns first, for example a for adults and c for children.\n\nYour task: (a) y = x + 2 and 2x + y = 11. (b) x + y = 12 and x − y = 2. (c) 2x + y = 10 and x − y = 2. (d) a + c = 100 and 8a + 5c = 620.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.9.1.", "video": "", "script": "Graphing systems is helpful, but when the answers are fractions it is hard to be exact. Today we learn two algebraic methods that always give exact answers: substitution and elimination.\n\nSubstitution works well when one equation already has a variable by itself. Take y equals two x minus one, and three x plus y equals nine. The first equation tells us that y is the same as two x minus one. So in the second equation we replace y with two x minus one. Three x plus the quantity two x minus one, equals nine. Combine like terms: five x minus one equals nine. Add one: five x equals ten. So x equals two. Now substitute back into the first equation to find y: two times two minus one is three. The solution is two comma three.\n\nElimination is best when the equations are in standard form. The idea is to add or subtract the equations so that one variable disappears. Take x plus y equals ten and x minus y equals four. Notice that the y terms are opposites. If we add the equations, the y's cancel. We get two x equals fourteen, so x equals seven. Substitute back into the first equation: seven plus y equals ten, so y equals three. The solution is seven comma three.\n\nWhat if no variable cancels? Multiply one or both equations by a number first to create opposites. For instance, if you have two x plus y equals ten and x minus y equals two, just add them directly. Three x equals twelve, so x is four, and y is two.\n\nAlways check your solution in both original equations. This catches arithmetic mistakes.\n\nSystems are perfect for word problems with two unknowns. Suppose adult tickets cost eight dollars and child tickets cost five dollars. A hundred tickets are sold for six hundred twenty dollars. Let a be adult tickets and c be child tickets. The first equation is a plus c equals one hundred. The second is eight a plus five c equals six hundred twenty. From the first, c equals one hundred minus a. Substitute: eight a plus five times the quantity one hundred minus a, equals six hundred twenty. That is eight a plus five hundred minus five a equals six hundred twenty. So three a equals one hundred twenty, and a equals forty. Then c equals sixty. Check: forty times eight is three hundred twenty, and sixty times five is three hundred. Together that is six hundred twenty.\n\nYour task today is to solve four systems, including the ticket problem. Then take the four question check.\n\nNotice how two pieces of information together reveal what one piece alone could not. In the church, too, we are stronger together. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.9.1"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$4.3 Solving Systems Algebraically$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 16, $j$4.4 Systems of Inequalities and Real World Constraints$j$, $j${"body": "A system of inequalities has several inequalities at once. Its solution set is the region where all the shaded regions overlap.\n\nTo graph: graph each inequality (boundary line, solid or dashed, shade), then find the overlap.\n\nWorked example: y > x and y < 5. A point such as (2, 3) satisfies both: 3 > 2 and 3 < 5.\n\nConstraints: many problems have limits. Example: a student spends at most $24 on notebooks ($3 each, n of them) and pens ($2 each, p of them). Then 3n + 2p ≤ 24, n ≥ 0 and p ≥ 0. The last two constraints exist because you cannot buy a negative number of items.\n\nA viable option is a point in the overlap that also makes sense in context, for example a whole number of items.\n\nThink it through: a system of inequalities describes a set of rules that must all be true at once. The solution is the overlap of the shaded regions. To test a point, check it in every inequality. Real situations add hidden rules. You cannot buy a negative number of notebooks, so n ≥ 0 and p ≥ 0. And you usually buy whole items, so only whole number points count. In the notebook problem, n = 6 and p = 3 gives 3(6) + 2(3) = 24, which exactly uses the budget. A viable option must satisfy all the inequalities and make sense in the story.\n\nYour task: (a) Which of (2, 3), (4, 3), (1, 6), (6, 1) satisfy both y > x and y < 5? (b) Write the constraints for the notebook and pen problem. (c) List two viable purchases.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.9.4, MA.912.AR.9.6.", "video": "", "script": "We have solved systems of equations, and we have graphed single inequalities with two variables. Now we put the two ideas together: systems of inequalities. A system of inequalities is two or more inequalities that must be true at the same time.\n\nThe solution of a system of inequalities is not a single point. It is a region, the overlap of all the shaded regions. To find it, graph each inequality on the same plane. For each one, draw the boundary line, dashed or solid, and shade the correct side. The solution is where all the shaded areas overlap.\n\nLet us look at a small example: y is greater than x, and y is less than five. The first inequality shades above the line y equals x. The second shades below the horizontal line y equals five. The overlap is a wedge shape between them. A point like two comma three is in the overlap, because three is greater than two and three is less than five. A point like four comma three is not, because three is not greater than four.\n\nNow, the most useful part: real world constraints. A constraint is a limit on what is possible. Suppose a student can spend at most twenty four dollars on supplies. Notebooks cost three dollars each and pens cost two dollars each. Let n be the number of notebooks and p the number of pens. The spending limit is three n plus two p is less than or equal to twenty four. Two more constraints are hidden in the situation. You cannot buy a negative number of items, so n is greater than or equal to zero, and p is greater than or equal to zero. Together, these three inequalities form a system.\n\nThe overlap region is called the feasible region, because every point in it is a feasible, or workable, choice. But we must also think about context. Since you cannot buy part of a pen, only points with whole number coordinates are viable. For example, four notebooks and four pens cost twelve plus eight, or twenty dollars, which is allowed. Eight notebooks and zero pens costs exactly twenty four. Ten notebooks would cost thirty, which is more than the limit.\n\nLuke tells us that a builder first sits down and counts the cost, whether he has enough to finish. Systems of inequalities are a way of counting the cost.\n\nYour task today is to test points against a system, write the constraints for a budget problem, and list two viable purchases. Then take the four question check.\n\nGood leaders know their limits and plan within them. Constraints are not enemies. They are the guide rails of wise planning. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.9.4, MA.912.AR.9.6"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$4.4 Systems of Inequalities and Real World Constraints$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'assessment', 4, $j$Assessment 4: Systems and Inequalities, Show Your Work$j$, $j${"prompt": "Show every step. (1) Solve by graphing: y = −x + 6 and y = 2x. (2) Solve by substitution: y = 4x − 3 and 2x + y = 9. (3) Solve by elimination: 3x + 2y = 16 and x − 2y = 0. (4) Graph y ≥ x − 2 and describe the line and shading. (5) A youth fundraiser sells small cups at $2 and large cups at $3. They sell 60 cups and collect $150. Write a system and find how many of each. (6) A church van holds at most 15 people. Adults (a) and children (c) both ride. Write the constraints, graph the feasible region, and list two viable groups.", "rubric": "Total 100. Items 1 to 4 are worth 14 each. Item 5 is worth 20: system s + l = 60 and 2s + 3l = 150 (8), solution 30 small and 30 large (8), checked (4). Item 6 is worth 24: constraints a + c ≤ 15, a ≥ 0, c ≥ 0 (10), graph (8), two viable groups (6)."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'assessment' and title = $j$Assessment 4: Systems and Inequalities, Show Your Work$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'discussion', 4, $j$Discussion 4: Two Things at Once$j$, $j${"prompt": "Describe a real situation where two conditions had to be true at the same time, such as a budget and a schedule. How could a system of equations or inequalities help? Respond in 100 to 150 words."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'discussion' and title = $j$Discussion 4: Two Things at Once$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 16, $j$Check 4.1: Graphing Linear Inequalities$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "For the inequality y ≥ 3x + 2, the boundary line is:", "options": ["Vertical", "Dashed", "Solid", "Not drawn"]}, {"q": "Which point is a solution of y > x + 1?", "options": ["(2, 3)", "(−2, −1)", "(1, 1)", "(0, 3)"]}, {"q": "To graph y < 2x − 4, you test (0, 0). What do you find?", "options": ["0 < −4 is true, so shade the origin side", "The line must be solid", "0 < −4 is false, so shade the side without the origin", "The test point lies on the line"]}, {"q": "Which inequality says 'the sum of x and y is at most 10'?", "options": ["x + y ≥ 10", "x + y ≤ 10", "x − y ≤ 10", "x + y < 10"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 4.1: Graphing Linear Inequalities$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,3,2,1}'::int[], array[$j$≥ includes the line, so it is solid.$j$,$j$3 > 0 + 1 is true. The other points fail.$j$,$j$0 < −4 is false.$j$,$j$At most means less than or equal to.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 4.1: Graphing Linear Inequalities$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 17, $j$Check 4.2: Solving Systems by Graphing$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "What is the solution of y = x + 1 and y = −x + 5?", "options": ["(1, 4)", "(0, 1)", "(3, 2)", "(2, 3)"]}, {"q": "Two lines are parallel. How many solutions does the system have?", "options": ["One solution", "Infinitely many solutions", "Two solutions", "No solution"]}, {"q": "Both equations describe the same line. How many solutions does the system have?", "options": ["Exactly two", "Infinitely many", "Exactly one", "None"]}, {"q": "Which ordered pair satisfies both x + y = 7 and x − y = 1?", "options": ["(4, 3)", "(5, 2)", "(6, 1)", "(3, 4)"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 4.2: Solving Systems by Graphing$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,3,1,0}'::int[], array[$j$At (2, 3): 3 = 2 + 1 and 3 = −2 + 5.$j$,$j$Parallel lines never meet.$j$,$j$Every point on the line is a solution.$j$,$j$4 + 3 = 7 and 4 − 3 = 1.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 4.2: Solving Systems by Graphing$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 18, $j$Check 4.3: Solving Systems Algebraically$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Solve the system y = x + 2 and 2x + y = 11.", "options": ["(3, 5)", "(2, 4)", "(4, 6)", "(5, 3)"]}, {"q": "Solve the system x + y = 12 and x − y = 2.", "options": ["(6, 6)", "(7, 5)", "(5, 7)", "(8, 4)"]}, {"q": "Solve the system 2x + y = 10 and x − y = 2.", "options": ["(4, 2)", "(2, 4)", "(5, 0)", "(3, 4)"]}, {"q": "Adult tickets cost $8 and child tickets cost $5. 100 tickets sold for $620. How many child tickets were sold?", "options": ["50", "40", "60", "70"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 4.3: Solving Systems Algebraically$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,1,0,2}'::int[], array[$j$2x + x + 2 = 11 gives x = 3, so y = 5.$j$,$j$Adding gives 2x = 14, so x = 7 and y = 5.$j$,$j$Adding gives 3x = 12, so x = 4 and y = 2.$j$,$j$a + c = 100 and 8a + 5c = 620 gives a = 40 and c = 60.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 4.3: Solving Systems Algebraically$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 19, $j$Check 4.4: Systems of Inequalities and Real World Constraints$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Which point satisfies both y > x and y < 5?", "options": ["(4, 3)", "(2, 3)", "(6, 1)", "(1, 6)"]}, {"q": "Notebooks cost $3 (n) and pens cost $2 (p). You can spend at most $24. Which inequality models the spending?", "options": ["3n + 2p ≥ 24", "2n + 3p ≤ 24", "3n + 2p ≤ 24", "n + p ≤ 24"]}, {"q": "The solution of a system of inequalities is:", "options": ["The test point", "Any one of the boundary lines", "The region where all the shaded regions overlap", "Only the corner points"]}, {"q": "Why add the constraints n ≥ 0 and p ≥ 0?", "options": ["You cannot buy a negative number of items", "Items must cost more than zero", "To make the line dashed", "The graph must be in a circle"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 4.4: Systems of Inequalities and Real World Constraints$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{1,2,2,0}'::int[], array[$j$For (2, 3): 3 > 2 and 3 < 5.$j$,$j$Total cost must be at most 24.$j$,$j$Every point in the overlap satisfies all the inequalities.$j$,$j$Negative amounts make no sense in context.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 4.4: Systems of Inequalities and Real World Constraints$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 20, $j$Unit Quiz 4: Systems and Inequalities in Two Variables$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Solve the system x + y = 9 and x − y = 3.", "options": ["(6, 3)", "(5, 4)", "(3, 6)", "(4, 5)"]}, {"q": "Solve the system y = 2x and y = x + 4.", "options": ["(4, 4)", "(2, 4)", "(4, 8)", "(8, 4)"]}, {"q": "The inequality y < 3x − 2 has a boundary line that is:", "options": ["Dashed", "Vertical", "Solid", "Horizontal"]}, {"q": "A system of two parallel lines with different intercepts has:", "options": ["Two solutions", "Infinitely many solutions", "One solution", "No solution"]}, {"q": "Solve the system y = 3x and x + y = 20.", "options": ["(4, 12)", "(10, 10)", "(15, 5)", "(5, 15)"]}, {"q": "Which phrase means ≥?", "options": ["Less than", "At most", "At least", "Fewer than"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 4: Systems and Inequalities in Two Variables$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,2,0,3,3,2}'::int[], array[$j$2x = 12, so x = 6 and y = 3.$j$,$j$2x = x + 4 gives x = 4 and y = 8.$j$,$j$Strict inequalities use a dashed line.$j$,$j$Parallel lines never intersect.$j$,$j$x + 3x = 20 gives x = 5 and y = 15.$j$,$j$At least means greater than or equal to.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 4: Systems and Inequalities in Two Variables$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
